pwgen
=====

pwgen is a script to generate pronounceable passwords in the same style
as classic pwgen, but with quantified entropy and unbiased distribution.


History
-------

Ted Ts'o's original pwgen generates passwords using a series of phonetic
heuristics, making them easier to read and memorise at the cost of reduced
entropy per character.

Unfortunately, it selects some passwords much more frequently than others.
The ad hoc algorithm is hard to investigate analytically, but for a specific
output, it's possible to take a dynamic programming approach, summing
probabilities across all the possible paths that might have produced it.

Doing this for 12-character pwgen phonetic outputs shows wildly varying
probabilities, with -log2(p) ranging from 59 right down to 35, compared
with 71.5 for uniform choices from [A-Za-z0-9]{12}. A 12-character password
with less entropy than a 6-character uniform one is not ideal.


Unbiased selection
------------------

We can fix this problem by choosing every permitted output with the
same probability.

First, we define some simple rules to achieve a similar result to the
original. Single or double vowels alternate with single consonants or
whitelisted consonant pairs to form words. Only the first letter of a
word can be capitalised, and words can only be interrupted by a digit
after at least three letters.

From a state representation of a partial password, moves() yields each
acceptable continuation character in turn, in deterministic order, with the
corresponding updated state. The resulting tree defines a total ordering
on permitted full-length passwords.

count() caches the number of full completions from a given state, i.e.
the size of any subtree. This enables us to traverse efficiently to any
leaf from its index in the ordering.

To generate an unbiased password, we just uniformly choose an index within
the ordering range, then traverse the tree to return the corresponding leaf.

This process results in 12-character and 16-character outputs with a
constant 53.3 and 71.1 bits of entropy respectively, compared with 71.5
and 95.3 bits for uniform passwords from the same alphabet.

An 11-character pronounceable password has slightly lower collision
probability than 8 characters selected uniformly from [A-Za-z0-9]{8}.
10-character output beats the 44-bit entropy described in xkcd 936's
"correct horse battery staple".

The time to generate fifty 12-character or 16-character passwords is
dominated by interpreter startup, so the algorithm is acceptably fast.


Timing side channel
-------------------

Initialising the count cache takes much more time than subsequently
traversing the tree to select an individual password. Nonetheless,
the number of moves examined is leaked in the execution time with
non-negligible magnitude: about 2% for 12-character passwords. This is
analogous to leaking the sum of the bytes in a uniform password.

It's hard to contrive uses for pwgen that expose timing to an attacker,
but this variable execution time implies you shouldn't try.


Variations
----------

The script is designed for easy modification, and to report entropy
correctly subject to those changes. However, moves() must always yield
each possible continuation at most once. Any repetition will bias the
distribution and break the entropy calculation.

From the baseline 53.3 bits of surprise in a 12-character output,
adding an extra symbol to the digits increases it by about 0.25 bits.
Eliminating digits altogether reduces it to 46.4 bits. In this case,
capitals contribute exactly one bit: the case of the first letter.

Allowing an extra consonant pair adds just 0.015 bits to a 12-character
password; dropping clusters entirely costs only 0.88 bits. It is arguable
whether this is worth the extra complexity they impose.

We don't implement consonant triples like 'str' because they considerably
enlarge the state space over which count() needs to cache, but experiments
suggest they add negligible entropy in any case.

With digits but no capitals, entropy is 50.7 bits. Case-folding standard
pwgen output is sufficient to obtain unbiased lower-case passwords.

The minimum word length between digits is quite significant. Varying this
from 2 to 3 to 4 gives surprise bits of 55.2, 53.3 and 52.0 respectively.


